package cli

import (
	"fmt"
	"os"
	"path/filepath"
	"strings"
)

// The skill is the agent-facing manual. It is generated by the binary rather than
// shipped as a static file so it cannot describe a command set this build does not
// have.
const skillName = "ftop"

// skillBody renders SKILL.md.
//
// Written for an agent that reads it once and then acts. Every rule is a decision the
// agent will actually face: take a card, find a conflict, discover a card that is busy
// but unclaimed. Anything else would dilute those.
// skillTemplate is the manual, with § standing for a backtick and {{version}} for
// this build's version.
//
// A raw Go string cannot contain a backtick, and the manual is mostly command lines,
// so the two substitutions are made once at render time rather than escaped on every
// line. A literal § in the text would be surprising; there is none.
const skillTemplate = `---
name: ftop
description: Which GPU cards are free and who holds them. Read before running anything that needs a GPU, and before ssh-ing to a machine to start an experiment. Claim the card first, release it when done.
---

# ftop

Machines here share GPUs, and other agents are using them now. ftop is the record of
who holds which card, and why. It never starts, stops or kills anything — the job is
always yours to run.

This client: §ftop {{version}}§ (check with §ftop version§).

## Every job, three steps

    ftop claim -n 2 -why "…" -for 3h       # ftop chooses the machine and the cards
    # run on data.hosts[0].host with CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=<data.env.CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES>
    ftop release <data.group>

§-why§ (a line a human understands) and §-for§ (a duration such as §90m§ or §3h§, at
most §720h§) are required. §-n§ is how many cards (default 1): you get all of them or
none.

Let ftop choose the machine. It observes the fleet, takes only cards it has just seen
idle, and puts the job where it fits most closely — two cards on a two-card machine,
not into an eight-card one; one card into the last free slot of a busy machine — so
whole machines stay free for the jobs that need them. §data.placement.reason§ says why.

- §-n N§ puts all N cards on one machine: one job across several cards.
- §-n N -spread§ lets them be on different machines: N separate one-card jobs, such as
  a sweep. §data.hosts§ then lists each machine with its own §cuda_visible_devices§.
- §-host <host>§ takes the cards on that machine, for when the operator told you to use
  it (record that with §-directive§, below). §-gpu <uuid>§ takes one exact card.
- §-min-vram <MiB>§ requires memory.

The reply carries §data.group§, §data.hosts§ (each machine, its cards and
§cuda_visible_devices§), §data.env§ (ready-to-use exports; §CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES§ when
the cards are on one machine) and §data.hint§. Keep the group: §ftop release <group>§
and §ftop renew <group>§ act on every card it took. Each card also has its own claim id
(§data.claims[].id§), to release one card of a spread when its job ends.

Too few idle cards fails with exit 3 and §error.code: "no_capacity"§, and nothing is
claimed. §error.conflicts§ says the most one machine could give (§most_on_one_host§,
§best_host§) and how many are idle in all (§idle§): ask for fewer, add §-spread§ if the
work really is separate one-card jobs, wait, or ask the operator.

Running longer than you said: §ftop renew <group> -for 4h§. Renewing is cheap and
better than letting the deadline pass.

After a restart, run §ftop claim list -mine§ before claiming again: the old claim is
still yours and still holding a card. §-mine§ lists exactly what you can release — your
own owner name, or the same runtime in the same repository and directory — so act on
what it shows. A claim you made with §-as <name>§ is found with §-as <name>§.
§-any-owner§ is the operator's and refuses to run without their marker.

To look before claiming, §ftop gpu list -safe§ lists the cards that are free and idle.
**§-safe§ is the listing to trust.** §-free§ only means no claim; it includes cards with
another job already running on them (§summary.unclaimed§). §-safe§ excludes them and
returns only cards the probe enumerated as empty. It needs an observation and refuses
§-no-ssh§, which is the point. §summary.safe§ is the same count.

A claim that names no host and §gpu list§ both contact every host, so they are the slow
commands: seconds on a warm fleet, and minutes on the first run after an upgrade while
the new probe is pushed to each machine. Do not run them in a loop or with a short
timeout.

## Tell ftop who you are

ftop records an owner on every claim so other agents can find you. It recognises some
runtimes by themselves; for anything else, set this once in your own instructions:

    export FTOP_AGENT=<your name>        # the tool or the job you run
    export FTOP_SESSION=<conversation>   # optional, but recommended

§ftop whoami§ shows what would be recorded. Without it a claim is refused: an
unidentified caller shares one owner with every other unidentified one.

The owner is §<agent>/<session>§, which is why a restart still finds its own claims.
§-as <name>§ overrides it — for a script, or one job that is not a conversation.

## If a card is taken

    ftop gpu show <uuid|host:index>   # who, why, their processes, when it is due back

The claim fails with exit 3 and §error.code: "held"§, naming the holder, their session,
their reason and — when the operator gave one — the instruction behind it. Read it
before doing anything else.

Two agents wanting one card usually means the operator told each of you something.
Settle it between yourselves; the operator is the last resort, not the first.

1. Read the holder's §why§ and §directive§.
2. Their §directive§ names this card or machine and yours does not: it is theirs.
   Take another card or wait.
3. Yours names it and theirs does not: ask them to release it. Say who you are and
   which instruction you were given; ftop has no message channel, so this goes through
   whatever channel you already share with them.
4. Neither or both name it: the earlier claim stands.

When the operator gives you a machine, record it, so the next agent can see why:

    ftop claim -host <host> -why "…" -for 3h -directive "use <host> for the sweep"

Two things not to do: release another agent's claim (§-any-owner§ is the operator's and
refuses to run without their marker), and kill a process because its claim looks stale.
Read the conflict and escalate.

A card the ledger calls free while the probe lists processes on it is a job started
outside ftop. ftop never hands it out: §claim -gpu§ refuses it with
§error.code: "not_idle"§. If those processes are yours, claim it with
§ftop claim -gpu <uuid> -adopt -why "…" -for 3h§; otherwise say so in your reply to the
operator and take another card.

## What the ledger means

- **A deadline is not an eviction.** Past §expires§ the claim still holds its card and
  is flagged §overdue§. It is closed only when ftop next observes that card and finds
  no process on it.
- **§summary§ is not a partition.** §cards§ is the total; §free§ and §held§ split it by
  the ledger. §unclaimed§ is a subset of §free§ (free cards with processes). §unknown§
  cuts across everything — it counts every card whose process list was not read, held
  ones included — so it is not a subset of §free§. §safe§ is the only count that answers
  "can I start now": free, enumerated, and empty. §retired§ counts cards that are not
  part of the fleet any more — their machine left §~/.ssh/config§, or the card left its
  host — and they are in no other count. A retired card that still carries a claim is
  listed, because someone is working on it.
- **§unknown§ is not free.** It counts cards whose process list could not be read,
  including every card under §-no-ssh§.
- **§unclaimed§** counts free cards that already have processes.
- **§conflict§** on a card means the ledger and the probe disagree. That is a question
  for a human, not an instruction to act.
- **§present: false§** means the card left its host's inventory: still in the history,
  no longer claimable. A §null§ claim means nobody holds the card.

## Commands

    ftop host                                        every machine and its label
    ftop gpu why [-host H]                           one line per card: who, and why
    ftop claim list [-mine]                          open claims, or just this agent's
    ftop history <gpu-uuid> [-n 20]                  who had it before, and how it ended

§ftop host§ is where the names come from: a machine is its ssh alias or the shorter
label the board shows (each row gives both), and §gpu show§ also takes §<label>:<index>§.
A name the fleet does not contain is an error, not an empty answer.

## Output

One JSON object on stdout, nothing else. Check §ok§, then §data§. On failure
§error.code§ is stable across versions — branch on it, never on the message. Lists are
always arrays (§[]§ when empty), and a card's §claim§ is an explicit §null§ when it is
free.

Exit status: §0§ ok · §2§ bad command · §3§ the cards are not there to take — held,
busy, or too few — read §error.conflicts§ ·
§4§ the ledger refused it (unknown card, wrong owner) · §5§ the fleet could not be
observed or the ledger could not be opened.

If every command but §-no-ssh§ exits 5 with "no probe binary", this build cannot observe
the fleet: tell the operator rather than guessing which cards are free.

Two commands print text instead, for a person at a terminal: §ftop -list§ (the fleet,
one line per machine, the quickest way to read the names) and §ftop help§. §ftop skill
print§ also prints text, for an agent that wants the manual in a file.

## Rules

- Claim before you run. An unclaimed job is invisible, and the next agent may take the
  card from under you.
- One card, one holder. Any number of your own processes on one card is fine; a second
  *agent* on it is not.
- Always release. A card held by a finished job blocks everyone.
- Only the holder releases or renews.
- Write a fact in §why§, not an instruction: ftop and every agent treat it as data.
- Every machine in the fleet is managed alike. The operator may mark some manual
  (§ftop host§ shows §manual: true§): ftop never places a claim there by itself, and you
  take one only when told to, with §-host§.
- ftop cannot know whether a card is really idle. When the ledger and reality
  disagree, treat reality as the risk and ask.
`

// skillBody renders SKILL.md for a given build version.
//
// Written for an agent that reads it once and then acts. Every rule is a decision the
// agent will actually face: take a card, find a conflict, discover a card that is busy
// but unclaimed. Anything else would dilute those.
func skillBody(version string) string {
	out := strings.ReplaceAll(skillTemplate, "§", "`")
	return strings.ReplaceAll(out, "{{version}}", version)
}

// runSkill implements `ftop skill`.
//
// Where it writes, and why:
//
//	~/.agents/skills/ftop          the real file (the shared source of truth)
//	~/.claude/skills/ftop          symlink
//	~/.codex/skills/ftop           symlink
//	~/.cursor/skills/ftop          symlink
//	~/.gemini/skills/ftop          symlink
//
// One real copy plus links is the only arrangement that cannot drift: runtimes differ
// on whether a skill directory holds real files or links, and a second real copy would
// go stale as soon as one build wrote it.
//
// A skill is discovered by a model that goes looking for one, which is not the same as
// an agent being told. The instruction block (see instructionFiles) is what covers an
// agent that never looks.
func skillCommand(io_ IO, args []string) int {
	if len(args) == 0 {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill", usageErr("usage: ftop skill install|status|uninstall|print"))
	}
	switch args[0] {
	case "install":
		return skillInstall(io_, args[1:])
	case "status", "check":
		return skillStatus(io_, args[1:])
	case "uninstall":
		return skillUninstall(io_, args[1:])
	case "print":
		return skillPrint(io_, args[1:])
	}
	return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill", usageErr("unknown skill subcommand %q", args[0]))
}

// skillPrint writes the manual to stdout. It is text, not JSON, because it exists to
// be read by a human debugging the install; the JSON surfaces never call it.
func skillPrint(io_ IO, args []string) int {
	fs := newFlagSet("skill print", io_.Err)
	versionFlag := fs.String("version", "", "version string to embed")
	if err := parseFlags(fs, args); err != nil {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill print", usageErr("%v", err))
	}
	v := *versionFlag
	if v == "" {
		v = BuildVersion
	}
	writeText(io_.Out, skillBody(v))
	return ExitOK
}

// BuildVersion is set by main from its link-time version.
var BuildVersion = "dev"

// skillRoots are the agent runtimes ftop links the manual into, by the directory each
// one reads user-level skills from.
//
// ~/.agents/skills is the interoperable location: Cursor and Gemini CLI read it
// directly, so they need no link at all, and it is where the real file lives. The
// others are per-runtime directories that have to be pointed at it. Adding a runtime
// is one line here; nothing else in the program names a vendor.
//
// Opencode has no user-level skills directory of its own and reads ~/.claude/skills,
// so it is covered by that link.
var skillRoots = []string{
	".claude/skills", // Claude Code, and opencode's Claude compatibility path
	".codex/skills",  // Codex
	".cursor/skills", // Cursor, in addition to ~/.agents/skills
	".gemini/skills", // Gemini CLI, in addition to ~/.agents/skills
}

// skillPaths is where the skill is installed, in order. The first entry is the real
// directory; the rest are links to it.
//
// The primary path is fixed (#1) rather than taken from the first link, so the file
// an agent edits is the same one every runtime sees.
func skillPaths() (primary string, links []string) {
	home, err := os.UserHomeDir()
	if err != nil {
		return "", nil
	}
	primary = filepath.Join(home, ".agents", "skills", skillName)
	for _, r := range skillRoots {
		// Only a runtime the user actually has: creating ~/.codex to hold a link
		// would leave an empty agent home behind on a machine that never ran it.
		root := strings.SplitN(filepath.ToSlash(r), "/", 2)[0]
		if _, err := os.Stat(filepath.Join(home, root)); err != nil {
			continue
		}
		links = append(links, filepath.Join(home, r, skillName))
	}
	return primary, links
}

// instructionFiles are the persistent instruction files each runtime reads at the
// start of every session, in the order they are checked.
//
// A skill is discovered by a model that goes looking for one. That is not the same as
// an agent being told, and an agent that has never heard of ftop has no reason to look
// for a GPU skill before it starts a job. So the manual is also pointed at from the
// file each runtime loads unconditionally, in a marked block that is the only thing
// ftop ever touches: no existing line is rewritten, and `skill uninstall` removes it.
//
// Runtimes that read no such file (Cursor, whose user rules live in its UI) are still
// served by the skill itself, which Cursor loads automatically at startup.
var instructionFiles = []struct {
	path string
	// shadow is a higher-precedence file that, when present, makes this one ignored.
	shadow string
	// exists gates the write: ftop does not create a home directory for a runtime the
	// user does not have.
	exists string
}{
	{path: ".claude/CLAUDE.md", exists: ".claude"},
	{path: ".codex/AGENTS.md", shadow: ".codex/AGENTS.override.md", exists: ".codex"},
	{path: ".gemini/GEMINI.md", exists: ".gemini"},
	{path: ".config/opencode/AGENTS.md", exists: ".config/opencode"},
}

const (
	blockBegin = "<!-- ftop:begin (managed by `ftop skill install`; do not edit inside) -->"
	blockEnd   = "<!-- ftop:end -->"
)

// instructionBlock is what is written into a runtime's global instruction file.
//
// Four lines, because it competes with everything else the user has told their agents
// and its only job is to make the agent look at the manual before touching a machine.
func instructionBlock() string {
	return blockBegin + "\n" +
		"# GPU fleet\n" +
		"\n" +
		"Machines in this environment share GPU cards, and other agents use them too.\n" +
		"Before you run anything that needs a GPU, or ssh to a machine from `~/.ssh/config`\n" +
		"to run an experiment, read the `ftop` skill (`~/.agents/skills/ftop/SKILL.md`) and\n" +
		"follow it: `ftop claim -n <cards> -why \"…\" -for <time>` picks idle cards for you; run\n" +
		"on exactly those, then release them.\n" +
		blockEnd + "\n"
}

// writeInstructionBlock puts the block into path, keeping everything already there.
//
// The block is replaced in place when it exists, appended otherwise, and the file is
// created only when its runtime directory is already present — installing ftop must
// not invent configuration for an agent the user does not run. An unreadable or
// unwritable file is reported, never silently skipped.
func writeInstructionBlock(path string) (skillEntry, bool) {
	e := skillEntry{Path: path, Kind: "instructions"}
	body, err := os.ReadFile(path)
	if err != nil && !os.IsNotExist(err) {
		e.Error = err.Error()
		return e, false
	}
	text := string(body)
	block := instructionBlock()
	switch {
	case strings.Contains(text, blockBegin):
		start := strings.Index(text, blockBegin)
		end := strings.Index(text[start:], blockEnd)
		if end < 0 {
			e.Kind, e.Error = "instructions", "the ftop block has no end marker; fix it by hand"
			return e, false
		}
		end += start + len(blockEnd)
		// Keep the newline that followed the old block, so the file does not lose a
		// blank line between sections.
		for end < len(text) && text[end] == '\n' {
			end++
		}
		text = text[:start] + block + text[end:]
	case text == "":
		text = block
	default:
		if !strings.HasSuffix(text, "\n") {
			text += "\n"
		}
		text += "\n" + block
	}
	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(path), 0o755); err != nil {
		e.Error = err.Error()
		return e, false
	}
	if err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(text), 0o644); err != nil {
		e.Error = err.Error()
		return e, false
	}
	e.OK = true
	return e, true
}

// stripInstructionBlock removes the block, leaving the rest of the file as it was.
// A file that becomes empty is removed, so an uninstall leaves nothing behind.
func stripInstructionBlock(path string) (skillEntry, bool) {
	e := skillEntry{Path: path, Kind: "instructions"}
	body, err := os.ReadFile(path)
	if os.IsNotExist(err) {
		e.OK = true
		e.Kind = "missing"
		return e, true
	}
	if err != nil {
		e.Error = err.Error()
		return e, false
	}
	text := string(body)
	start := strings.Index(text, blockBegin)
	if start < 0 {
		e.OK = true
		e.Kind = "missing"
		return e, true
	}
	end := strings.Index(text[start:], blockEnd)
	if end < 0 {
		e.Error = "the ftop block has no end marker; fix it by hand"
		return e, false
	}
	end += start + len(blockEnd)
	for end < len(text) && text[end] == '\n' {
		end++
	}
	text = text[:start] + text[end:]
	// The block is appended after a blank separator line, so removing it leaves that
	// separator at the end of the user's text. Trimming to exactly one trailing newline
	// makes the file byte-identical to what it was before install.
	text = strings.TrimRight(text, "\n")
	if text != "" {
		text += "\n"
	}
	if strings.TrimSpace(text) == "" {
		if err := os.Remove(path); err != nil {
			e.Error = err.Error()
			return e, false
		}
		e.Kind = "removed"
		e.OK = true
		return e, true
	}
	if err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(text), 0o644); err != nil {
		e.Error = err.Error()
		return e, false
	}
	e.OK = true
	return e, true
}

// instructionTargets lists the instruction files that would be written, given what
// the user actually has installed.
func instructionTargets() []string {
	home, err := os.UserHomeDir()
	if err != nil {
		return nil
	}
	var paths []string
	for _, f := range instructionFiles {
		if _, err := os.Stat(filepath.Join(home, f.exists)); err != nil {
			continue
		}
		p := filepath.Join(home, f.path)
		if f.shadow != "" {
			if _, err := os.Stat(filepath.Join(home, f.shadow)); err == nil {
				// The override file is what the runtime reads, so that is the file to
				// put the block in.
				p = filepath.Join(home, f.shadow)
			}
		}
		paths = append(paths, p)
	}
	return paths
}

type skillStatusData struct {
	Version     string       `json:"version"`
	PrimaryPath string       `json:"primary_path"`
	Installed   bool         `json:"installed"`
	Entries     []skillEntry `json:"entries"`
	// UpToDate is false when the installed manual differs from what this build would
	// write. It compares the whole text, not the version string: the manual changed
	// substantially between two builds that both said 0.3.0, and a version-only check
	// kept reporting the old manual as current.
	// build. That is the only signal an agent gets that its instructions are stale.
	UpToDate bool `json:"up_to_date"`
	// Method is how it was installed: "ftop" or "npx-skills". Both are supported;
	// npx puts the package under ~/.agents and links it the same way.
	Method string `json:"method,omitempty"`
}

type skillEntry struct {
	Path    string `json:"path"`
	Kind    string `json:"kind"` // directory, symlink, missing, wrong-target
	Target  string `json:"target,omitempty"`
	Version string `json:"version,omitempty"`
	OK      bool   `json:"ok"`
	Error   string `json:"error,omitempty"`
}

// skillInstall writes the manual and links it.
func skillInstall(io_ IO, args []string) int {
	fs := newFlagSet("skill install", io_.Err)
	versionFlag := fs.String("version", "", "version to embed")
	if err := parseFlags(fs, args); err != nil {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill install", usageErr("%v", err))
	}
	v := *versionFlag
	if v == "" {
		v = BuildVersion
	}

	primary, links := skillPaths()
	if primary == "" {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill install", stateErr(CodeInternal, "cannot determine home directory"))
	}
	if err := os.MkdirAll(primary, 0o755); err != nil {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill install", stateErr(CodeInternal, "%v", err))
	}
	body := skillBody(v)
	if err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(primary, "SKILL.md"), []byte(body), 0o644); err != nil {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill install", stateErr(CodeInternal, "%v", err))
	}

	entries := []skillEntry{{
		Path: primary, Kind: "directory", Version: v, OK: true,
	}}
	for _, link := range links {
		e := linkSkill(primary, link)
		entries = append(entries, e)
	}
	// The file each runtime reads unconditionally, so an agent that never went
	// looking for a skill still learns that it has to claim a card first.
	for _, path := range instructionTargets() {
		e, _ := writeInstructionBlock(path)
		entries = append(entries, e)
	}

	data := skillStatusData{
		Version: v, PrimaryPath: primary, Installed: true,
		Entries: entries, UpToDate: true, Method: "ftop",
	}
	failed := false
	for _, e := range entries {
		if !e.OK {
			failed = true
		}
	}
	if failed {
		// The manual is installed where it counts; a missing link means one agent
		// will not see it. Report that plainly rather than reporting success, and
		// carry the per-path entries in the error: telling a caller to "see
		// entries[].error" in an envelope that has no entries is worse than saying
		// nothing, because it makes the caller look in a place that does not exist.
		var bad []string
		for _, e := range entries {
			if !e.OK {
				bad = append(bad, e.Path+": "+e.Error)
			}
		}
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill install", &ExitError{Status: ExitState, API: &APIError{
			Code:      CodeInternal,
			Message:   "skill written, but one or more links failed: " + strings.Join(bad, "; "),
			Hint:      "the manual is at " + primary + "; fix or remove the paths above and rerun",
			Conflicts: entries,
		}})
	}
	emitOK(io_.Out, "skill install", data)
	return ExitOK
}

// skillUninstall removes the pointers ftop added: the marked block in each
// instruction file, the links, and the manual itself. Nothing else in those files is
// touched.
func skillUninstall(io_ IO, args []string) int {
	fs := newFlagSet("skill uninstall", io_.Err)
	if err := parseFlags(fs, args); err != nil {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill uninstall", usageErr("%v", err))
	}
	primary, links := skillPaths()
	if primary == "" {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill uninstall", stateErr(CodeInternal, "cannot determine home directory"))
	}
	entries := make([]skillEntry, 0, len(links)+len(instructionFiles)+1)
	for _, path := range instructionTargets() {
		e, _ := stripInstructionBlock(path)
		entries = append(entries, e)
	}
	for _, link := range links {
		e := skillEntry{Path: link}
		if fi, err := os.Lstat(link); err == nil && fi.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {
			if err := os.Remove(link); err != nil {
				e.Error = err.Error()
				e.Kind = "symlink"
			} else {
				e.Kind, e.OK = "removed", true
			}
		} else if err == nil {
			// A real directory is not ours to delete.
			e.Kind, e.Error = "directory", "a real directory exists here; not removing it"
		} else {
			e.Kind, e.OK = "missing", true
		}
		entries = append(entries, e)
	}
	if err := os.RemoveAll(primary); err != nil {
		entries = append(entries, skillEntry{Path: primary, Kind: "directory", Error: err.Error()})
	} else {
		entries = append(entries, skillEntry{Path: primary, Kind: "removed", OK: true})
	}
	emitOK(io_.Out, "skill uninstall", skillStatusData{
		Version: BuildVersion, PrimaryPath: primary, Installed: false, Entries: entries,
	})
	return ExitOK
}

// linkSkill makes link point at primary, without ever replacing a real directory
// someone else put there.
func linkSkill(primary, link string) skillEntry {
	e := skillEntry{Path: link, Target: primary}
	if fi, err := os.Lstat(link); err == nil {
		switch {
		case fi.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0:
			if dest, err := os.Readlink(link); err == nil {
				e.Target = dest
				e.Kind = "symlink"
				// A link that already points here is the desired state.
				if dest == primary {
					e.OK = true
					e.Target = primary
					return e
				}
			}
		default:
			// A real directory: leave it. Overwriting would destroy whatever the
			// user or another installer put there.
			e.Kind = "directory"
			e.Error = "a real directory exists here; not replacing it"
			return e
		}
		// Dangling or foreign symlink: ours to fix.
		if err := os.Remove(link); err != nil {
			e.Kind = "wrong-target"
			e.Error = err.Error()
			return e
		}
	}
	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(link), 0o755); err != nil {
		e.Kind = "missing"
		e.Error = err.Error()
		return e
	}
	if err := os.Symlink(primary, link); err != nil {
		e.Kind = "missing"
		e.Error = err.Error()
		return e
	}
	e.Kind = "symlink"
	e.OK = true
	return e
}

// skillStatus reports whether the manual is installed and current.
func skillStatus(io_ IO, args []string) int {
	fs := newFlagSet("skill status", io_.Err)
	if err := parseFlags(fs, args); err != nil {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill status", usageErr("%v", err))
	}
	primary, links := skillPaths()
	data := skillStatusData{Version: BuildVersion, PrimaryPath: primary}
	if primary == "" {
		return emitErr(io_.Out, "skill status", stateErr(CodeInternal, "cannot determine home directory"))
	}
	path := filepath.Join(primary, "SKILL.md")
	body, err := os.ReadFile(path)
	if err != nil {
		data.Installed = false
		data.Entries = append(data.Entries, skillEntry{
			Path: primary, Kind: "missing", Error: "not installed",
		})
		emitOK(io_.Out, "skill status", data)
		return ExitOK
	}
	data.Installed = true
	data.Version = skillEmbeddedVersion(string(body))
	data.UpToDate = string(body) == skillBody(BuildVersion)
	data.Entries = append(data.Entries, skillEntry{
		Path: primary, Kind: "directory", Version: data.Version, OK: true,
	})
	for _, path := range instructionTargets() {
		e := skillEntry{Path: path, Kind: "instructions"}
		body, err := os.ReadFile(path)
		switch {
		case err != nil:
			e.OK = false
			e.Kind = "missing"
			e.Error = "no ftop block; run `ftop skill install`"
		case strings.Contains(string(body), blockBegin):
			e.OK = true
		default:
			e.OK = false
			e.Error = "no ftop block; run `ftop skill install`"
		}
		data.Entries = append(data.Entries, e)
	}
	for _, link := range links {
		e := skillEntry{Path: link}
		if fi, err := os.Lstat(link); err == nil {
			if fi.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {
				e.Kind = "symlink"
				if dest, err := os.Readlink(link); err == nil {
					e.Target = dest
					e.OK = dest == primary
				}
			} else {
				e.Kind = "directory"
				e.OK = false
				e.Error = "not a symlink to the shared copy"
			}
		} else {
			e.Kind = "missing"
			e.Error = "not linked"
		}
		data.Entries = append(data.Entries, e)
	}
	emitOK(io_.Out, "skill status", data)
	return ExitOK
}

// skillEmbeddedVersion reads the version line out of an installed manual.
//
// The marker is the whole "`ftop X`" span, because the line reads
// "… `ftop 0.3.0`." — matching a bare "ftop `" found nothing and reported every
// install as stale.
func skillEmbeddedVersion(body string) string {
	// The first "`ftop " in the manual is inside the front matter's example; the
	// version line is the first one followed by a closing backtick.
	const marker = "`ftop "
	i := strings.Index(body, marker)
	if i < 0 {
		return ""
	}
	rest := body[i+len(marker):]
	j := strings.Index(rest, "`")
	if j < 0 {
		return ""
	}
	return strings.TrimSpace(rest[:j])
}

// SkillSummary is what the TUI shows on its footer: one line, no JSON.
//
// It reports coverage rather than the version alone. A manual that is installed but
// pointed at from no runtime's instruction file is one no agent will be told about,
// and saying "installed" to that would be the wrong answer.
func SkillSummary() string {
	primary, links := skillPaths()
	if primary == "" {
		return "skill: unknown"
	}
	body, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(primary, "SKILL.md"))
	if err != nil {
		return "skill not installed — press s to install"
	}
	v := skillEmbeddedVersion(string(body))
	if string(body) != skillBody(BuildVersion) {
		// Same version, different text is still stale: say "outdated" rather than
		// naming two identical version numbers as if they differed.
		if v == BuildVersion {
			return "skill is outdated — press s to update"
		}
		return fmt.Sprintf("skill %s is stale (ftop %s) — press s", v, BuildVersion)
	}
	linked := 0
	for _, l := range links {
		if fi, err := os.Lstat(l); err == nil && fi.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {
			linked++
		}
	}
	pointed, total := 0, 0
	for _, path := range instructionTargets() {
		total++
		if b, err := os.ReadFile(path); err == nil && strings.Contains(string(b), blockBegin) {
			pointed++
		}
	}
	if total > 0 && pointed == 0 {
		// The worst state this line can report: the manual exists and nothing tells an
		// agent to read it.
		return "skill not linked to any agent — press s"
	}
	if total == 0 {
		return fmt.Sprintf("skill %s installed, no agent found — press s", v)
	}
	return fmt.Sprintf("skill %s · %d link(s) · %d/%d agent(s)", v, linked, pointed, total)
}
